# {{ template "chart.name" . }}

{{ template "chart.description" . }} Chart version {{ template "chart.version" . }}, app version {{ template "chart.appVersion" . }}.

This README is generated by `helm-docs` from `README.md.gotmpl` and the comments in `values.yaml`. Edit those, then run `scripts/chart-check.sh docs-update`; do not edit the values table by hand.

## Status

**This chart is not published.** There is no OCI chart on GHCR and no Helm repository. Install it from a checkout of this repository, as shown below. The container image (`ghcr.io/cyberglitchlabs/patina`) is published; the chart that deploys it is not.

## Install

```sh
git clone https://github.com/cyberglitchlabs/patina
cd patina
helm install patina charts/patina -n patina --create-namespace -f my-values.yaml
```

The namespace is whatever you pass to `-n`; the chart does not create one. Minimal `my-values.yaml`:

```yaml
config: |
  timezone = "America/Chicago"

  [[providers]]
  kind = "gracenote"
  name = "gracenote"
  daily_budget = 150

  [[providers.lineups]]
  name = "home"
  lineup_id = "USA-OTA55401"
  postal_code = "55401"
  country = "USA"

  [[sources]]
  kind = "hdhomerun"
  url = "http://192.168.1.50"
  lineups = ["home"]
```

`helm install` prints the commands to read the tokens and to reach the service (`kubectl port-forward`). The keys of `config` are documented in [`config.example.toml`](../../config.example.toml), and the settings and endpoints in the [README](../../README.md) and [`docs/operations.md`](../../docs/operations.md).

## What the chart creates

- A Deployment with **one replica** and the `Recreate` strategy. Patina runs a single scheduler, so the schema rejects any `replicaCount` other than 1. `Recreate` is needed because the SQLite volume is ReadWriteOnce: the old pod must stop before the new one starts.
- `terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 90`. After SIGTERM Patina finishes the guide window it is merging, which can take up to a 60 s provider request timeout plus the merge itself. A shorter grace period risks a SIGKILL.
- A non-root pod (user 65532, `fsGroup` 65532) with a read-only root filesystem. `/data` is the only writable path. There is deliberately no `/tmp` emptyDir: SQLite falls back to the working directory, which is `/data`.
- A ConfigMap with `patina.toml` (and `overrides.toml` when `overrides` is set), a Service, and a Secret with the tokens unless you bring your own.
- Optionally an Ingress, a ServiceMonitor and a NetworkPolicy (see below).

The chart sets `PATINA_CONFIG`, and the image sets the listen address and the database URL, which override the TOML file.

## Configuration (`config`)

`config` is the contents of the TOML file as a plain string, not structured values. A string keeps numbers as integers (Helm turns YAML numbers into floats, which Patina rejects) and lets you paste `config.example.toml` as is.

- The chart rejects a `config` that is not valid TOML, or that sets `listen` or `database_url`: the chart owns both. Use `database.urlSecret` for Postgres.
- A config change restarts the pod (a checksum annotation).
- `overrides` holds the channel override file. It is mounted at `/etc/patina/overrides.toml`, and `config` must name it: set `overrides_path = "/etc/patina/overrides.toml"` on the `[[sources]]` entry that uses it. The chart fails otherwise.

## Tokens

Patina has a feed token and an admin token (see [operations](../../docs/operations.md#tokens)).

**Generated (default).** With `existingSecret` empty the chart creates a Secret named after the release with `PATINA_FEED_TOKEN` and `PATINA_ADMIN_TOKEN`, each 48 random alphanumeric characters. They are kept across `helm upgrade` by looking up the existing Secret. Read them with:

```sh
kubectl -n patina get secret patina -o jsonpath='{.data.PATINA_FEED_TOKEN}' | base64 -d; echo
kubectl -n patina get secret patina -o jsonpath='{.data.PATINA_ADMIN_TOKEN}' | base64 -d; echo
```

(The Secret is named `<release>-patina` if the release name does not contain `patina`; `helm install` prints the exact commands.) Because the lookup needs a cluster, offline `helm template` and `helm diff` without a reachable cluster show fresh random tokens each time. That is expected, not drift.

**Bring your own (`existingSecret`).** Create a Secret in the release namespace with both keys and name it in `existingSecret`:

```sh
kubectl -n patina create secret generic patina-tokens \
  --from-literal=PATINA_FEED_TOKEN="$(openssl rand -hex 24)" \
  --from-literal=PATINA_ADMIN_TOKEN="$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
```

```yaml
existingSecret: patina-tokens
```

The whole Secret is loaded as environment variables (`envFrom`), so a missing key is not an error: the pod starts and that endpoint is **open**. A key that is present but empty is treated as unset too, and startup warns about it. Patina logs `endpoint authentication` as `enabled` or `disabled` for each token; check it after install.

## Storage

- **Default:** a PersistentVolumeClaim for `/data` (SQLite). Set `persistence.size` and `persistence.storageClass` as needed.
- **Existing claim:** `persistence.existingClaim` mounts a claim you manage; the chart creates none.
- **No persistence:** `persistence.enabled=false` uses an emptyDir. The guide is lost whenever the pod restarts and is rebuilt from the providers (which spends request budget). Use it for trials only.
- **Postgres:** set `database.urlSecret.name` (and `key`, default `PATINA_DATABASE_URL`) to a Secret holding the connection URL, for example `postgres://patina:password@db.example:5432/patina?sslmode=require`. The chart then creates no PVC and `/data` is an emptyDir for scratch files. Setting both `database.urlSecret.name` and `persistence.existingClaim` fails the render.

## Optional extras

- **Ingress** (`ingress.enabled`): set `className`, `hosts` and `tls`, for example host `patina.example.com`. Patina speaks plain HTTP, and feed URLs carry the feed token, so use TLS.
- **ServiceMonitor** (`serviceMonitor.enabled`): needs the Prometheus Operator CRDs in the cluster. `/metrics` requires the admin token as a bearer token, so the ServiceMonitor reads key `PATINA_ADMIN_TOKEN` from the token Secret (generated or `existingSecret`); that key must exist. Set `serviceMonitor.labels` to match your Prometheus selector.
- **NetworkPolicy** (`networkPolicy.enabled`): allows ingress to the pod from the peers in `networkPolicy.ingressFrom`. An empty list denies all ingress, which includes Jellyfin and Prometheus, so list them. The policy has no egress rules, so egress is not restricted.

## Upgrade and uninstall

```sh
helm upgrade patina charts/patina -n patina -f my-values.yaml
helm uninstall patina -n patina
```

Upgrades keep generated tokens. Uninstall deletes the PVC the chart created (and with it the SQLite guide); a claim from `existingClaim` is never touched.

## Development

`scripts/chart-check.sh` runs what CI runs, so run it before and after any change under `charts/patina`:

```sh
scripts/chart-check.sh all      # lint, unittest, kubeconform, trivy, tokens, docs
```

| Function | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `lint` | `helm lint --strict` on every `ci/*-values.yaml` scenario |
| `unittest` | `helm unittest` suites in `tests/` |
| `kubeconform` | renders each scenario and validates it against the pinned Kubernetes and ServiceMonitor schemas |
| `trivy` | `trivy config` on each scenario, failing on HIGH and CRITICAL |
| `tokens` | checks that the generated tokens are two distinct 48-character alphanumeric strings |
| `docs` | fails if this README differs from what `helm-docs` generates |
| `docs-update` | regenerates this README |
| `install` | installs the bootable scenarios in a kind cluster and checks that `helm upgrade` keeps the tokens; needs kind and a local `patina:ci` image, so `all` does not run it |
| `pins` | prints the pinned tool versions as `KEY=value` lines |

The tools and their pinned versions live at the top of the script (Renovate bumps them); the script prints the installed version and fails on a major mismatch. The `Chart` workflow (`.github/workflows/chart.yml`) runs each function as its own job, plus `ct lint`. The SARIF upload to code scanning and the `ct` chart version bump check only do real work on a pull request, so they first run when you open one.

Any change under `charts/patina` needs a `version` bump in `Chart.yaml`; `ct` enforces it on pull requests.

